 Discard B1 or B2 or B3: Breaks potential chow, but could aim for pung-based hand.  \n- Goal: To win, you need at least 8 points. Patterns possible:  \n  - Half Flush (6) + Dragon Pung? (No dragons) + Seat Wind Pung? (If you get F1 pung) + All Pungs? (If you get two more pungs) + Kong? (B9 kong) + etc.  \n  - Alternatively, try for \"All Pungs\" (6) + \"Half Flush\" (6) = 12 points already.  \n\nReason:  \n- The safest discard now is to keep the B1,B2,B3 sequence because it offers flexibility for chow or pung. Discarding F1 might be premature because F1 is your seat wind; if you draw another F1, you get a pung worth 2 points (Seat Wind). But currently you only have one F1, and the unshown F1 count is 1 left, so chance is low.  \n- However, discarding B7 seems better: B7 is isolated, no immediate sequence or pung potential (you have one B7, unshown B7 count 2, so pung chance moderate). Keeping B1,B2,B3 gives more options.  \n- Also, B9 triple is valuable: could become kong (adds points) or pung.  \n- So discard B7 to maintain hand flexibility and wait for draws to complete pungs (B9, F1, or others).  \n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
